.SH A) OVERVIEW
masking allows the same string comparision steps to check different parts of a
string at different times increasing reuse rather than overlapping and unique
tests.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
masks are simply saved strings that effect the finan string comparisions by
greying-out areas not to be compared with greyboxes (¬).

.SH C) EXAMPLE
mask-steps have six fields, but do not use them all.  the leading spaces are
changed to spacers (·) in this manual for clarity.

   verb------- § desc- § --func-- § param § --test-- § ---expect------------ §
   ·····mask   § set   § - - - -  § 1     § - - - -  § å·····¬¬¬¬¬·····¬æ    §
   ·····get    § check § yLOG_out § 13    § w_mask/1 § åsix  31258 bot 5æ    §
   ·····unmask § unset § - - - -  § ·     § - - - -  § ·                     §

.SH D) VERB
the verbs MASK and UNMASK are is expected to be indented five (5) characters
off-estting it from COND and later steps.  the verb field are is twelve (12)
characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb------- § desc- § --func-- § param § --test-- § ---expect------------ §
   ·····mask   § ·     § ·        § ·     § ·        § ·                     §
   ·····unmask § ·     § ·        § ·     § ·        § ·                     §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

since descriptions are demonstrated elsewhere, i have truncated them greatly
to fit everything in the available space ;)

   verb------- § desc- § --func-- § param § --test-- § ---expect------------ §
   ·····mask   § set   § ·        § ·     § ·        § ·                     §
   ·····unmask § unset § ·        § ·     § ·        § ·                     §

