   ·····unmask § ·     § ·        § ·     § - - - -  § ·                     §

.SH I) EXPECT
the sixth field is the expected results, in this case for MASK it is the
actual masking to be overlain on the exected results when used.  each expected
result can have greyboxes (¬) to mask columns and the mask is additive to that.
any non-greybox in he mask field is ignored.  these other characters just help
audit the masking by providing context.

   verb------- § desc- § --func-- § param § --test-- § ---expect------------ §
   ·····mask   § ·     § ·        § ·     § ·        § å·····¬¬¬¬¬·····¬æ    §
   ·····unmask § ·     § ·        § ·     § ·        § ·                     §

.SH J) USAGE
masks are typically used in GET or ECHO comparisons, but can be about anywhere.
to use one to replace s_equal or w_equal, use s_mask/# or w_mask/# as the test.

   verb------- § desc- § --func-- § param § --test-- § ---expect------------ §
   ·····mask   § set   § - - - -  § 1     § - - - -  § å·····¬¬¬¬¬·····¬æ    §
   ·····get    § check § yLOG_out § 13    § w_mask/1 § åsix  31258 bot 5æ    §

   the end results will then be compared to this --->  åsix  ¬¬¬¬¬ bot ¬æ

.SH K) OUTPUT
the result of the mask output is like most system calls, just a recitation
of the facts with no scores.

  ···ac) MASK   : set  ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·º18625»
  ·······mask   :  18åå·····¬¬¬¬¬·····¬ææ

the result of the comparision that used the mask is exactly as normal, but
with the final masking overlain on the expected result.

